{
 "question": "Does this decoder read a delta bit allocation that moves the masking curve as other decoders read it, and what does each do with the delta fields the standard forbids, reserves or describes twice?",
 "method": "crates/oadec-synth was given a writer of AC-3 syncframes: bsid 8, two channels, no coupling, long blocks, exponents sent one by one or reused, dithflag 0. A block is told its exponents and what each mantissa is to be; the bit allocation of clause 6.2.2 of ETSI TS 102 366 is worked out a second time, with the delta of clause 6.2.2.6, from tables a test holds equal to the decoder's; each mantissa is quantised to the quantiser its pointer names. The samples a stream comes to are the inverse transform of clause 6.9.4 written from its definition, in double precision. tools/synth_accept.py wrote every stream with `oadec-synth all` and decoded each with FFmpeg (`-drc_scale 0 -f ac3`, to 32-bit float), with the dlbac3dec element of the Dolby Reference Player (`drc-suppress=true out-ch-config=0`, to 32-bit float) and with `oadec decode --format wav`, each as a program on a file, and compared what it wrote with the writer's samples channel by channel as a signal-to-noise ratio, at no delay and at 32 samples. A decoder gave the samples when both channels were over 80 dB. A second front end to Dolby's decoder, the engine the Plex transcoder drives, was run on the same streams by a script outside the repository. The mutations are entries of tools/mutants.py, run with every test of the workspace against the tree of main and against this one, and against this one with only `cargo test -p oadec-synth --test ac3` and `cargo test -p oadec-cli --test cli_synth`.",
