  "not_measured": "the delta of the coupling channel (`cpldeltbae`), which needs coupling the writer does not write; E-AC-3 frames, which switch the fields on with `dbaflde`; more than two channels, an LFE, grouped exponents, short blocks and rematrixing. What Dolby's decoder does with a \"reuse\" after \"none\" was seen from outside only: it decodes the frames laid out with no delta and none of the frames laid out with one."
 },
 "reading": "All 8 references decode in FFmpeg's decoder and in Dolby's to the samples the writer says they come to, and in this decoder to every coefficient: an applied delta bit allocation is read the same way by three decoders, at three sampling rates, with one to eight segments up to the last band. A stream that keeps its delta to itself decodes in none of them, so the agreement is about the delta and not about the frames around it. The distance of Dolby's decoder from the writer, 103 to 138 dB, is the precision of five tables and nothing else: with the levels of the symmetric quantisers rounded to fifteen fractional bits it is at 138.5 dB or over everywhere. Where the standard can be read two ways the decoders part: after a \"none\", a \"reuse\" applies the stored segments in FFmpeg's decoder and in this one, as the pseudo code has it, and keeps \"none\" in Dolby's, as the table of the field has it. No stream examined has the sequence. Three more things have no rule in `verify` and decode without a word: a \"reuse\" in a first block, the reserved code and a segment past the last band. Each is refused or given up by at least one of the two outside decoders. Of 18 mutations of the AC-3 path, 15 survived the tests as they were, 0 survive now, and the tests over the written streams catch every one by themselves."
}
